 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T21:28+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, you should strongly consider staying indoors today.** \n\nThe Air Quality Index (AQI) in Kuala Lumpur is currently in the **\"Unhealthy\"** range (with high concentrations of fine particulate matter, or PM2.5). \n\n### Why this matters for your asthma:\n* **Airway Irritation:** Fine particles (PM2.5) are small enough to pass deep into your lungs. For someone with asthma, these pollutants act as strong irritants, causing inflammation, coughing, wheezing, and shortness of breath.\n* **Increased Risk of Attacks:** High pollution levels dramatically increase the risk of triggering an asthma flare-up or a severe attack, even with limited exposure.\n\n### Recommended Precautions:\n* **Stay Indoors:** Limit your time outside and keep windows and doors closed to prevent polluted air from entering your living space. If you have an air purifier with a HEPA filter, run it indoors.\n* **Avoid Outdoor Exertion:** Refrain from exercising or doing heavy physical activity outside. \n* **Keep Your Inhaler Close:** Ensure your rescue inhaler (reliever) is easily accessible at all times. \n* **Wear a Mask if You Must Go Out:** If you absolutely have to step outside, wear a well-fitted particulate respirator mask (such as an N95 or KN95) rather than a loose cloth or surgical mask, as standard masks do not block PM2.5.\n\n**Seek medical attention immediately** if you experience increased shortness of breath, chest tightness, coughing that won't stop, or if your rescue inhaler does not provide adequate relief.",
